At `eb0574d5528babd3b4aadece81590d8e2230b99b`, JavaScript async functions are preserved as carried source when targeting Rust. This blocks execution, retry-delay and failure-reporting adapters in [start#197](https://github.com/link-foundation/start/issues/197).

Minimal reproduction with Bun and installed `js/` dependencies:

```js
import { selfTranslate } from './js/src/self-translation.js';
const source = 'export async function execute(run) { return await run(); }\n';
const result = selfTranslate(source, 'JavaScript', 'Rust');
console.log(result.items.map(({term, status, reason}) => ({term, status, reason})));
console.log(result.code);
```

Actual: `export_statement` is `carried` with reason `unsupported`; output Rust contains only original JavaScript comments. Expected: supported typed async lowering, or an explicit diagnostic identifying asynchronous semantics and required callback signature/runtime contracts.

Workaround: keep native async/process adapters manually inventoried and check their behavior against common fixtures; generate only pure typed synchronous helpers. Original source preserved in comments must not count as generated executable behavior.

Suggested fix: recognize async/await in function declarations, require an explicit supported Future/callback signature, lower to native Rust async only when its runtime and error semantics are declared, and carry with an async-specific reason otherwise. Add executable async success/rejection/cancellation fixtures. #213 addresses signature inference generally; async syntax and scheduling/error semantics need separate support. Reproduction results are saved in [start#204](https://github.com/link-foundation/start/pull/204) under `docs/case-studies/issue-197/data/translation-probe.json`.

`link-foundation/start` is adopting JavaScript-first generation in [start#197](https://github.com/link-foundation/start/issues/197). At meta-language commit `eb0574d5528babd3b4aadece81590d8e2230b99b`, native Node/Bun runtime adapters and CommonJS module boundaries remain carried source, preventing generation of the process, filesystem and isolation core.

Reproduction with Bun, after installing `js/` dependencies at that commit:

```js
import { selfTranslate } from './js/src/self-translation.js';
for (const source of [
  `import { spawnSync } from 'node:child_process';\nexport function run() { return spawnSync('echo', ['hello']); }\n`,
  `const fs = require('node:fs');\nfunction read(path) { return fs.readFileSync(path, 'utf8'); }\nmodule.exports = { read };\n`,
]) {
  const result = selfTranslate(source, 'JavaScript', 'Rust');
  console.log(result.items.map(({ term, status, reason }) => ({ term, status, reason })));
}
```

Observed: the Node import is `carried/unsupported`, its exported function is `carried/type`; CommonJS require is `carried/unsupported`, function `carried/type`, `module.exports` `carried/unsupported`. Expected: an explicit portable adapter contract with supported typed native bindings, or a specific adapter blocker diagnostic. The actual finite probe and results are being preserved under `docs/case-studies/issue-197` in [start#204](https://github.com/link-foundation/start/pull/204).

Workaround: keep OS-dependent Rust adapters manually maintained and inventoried, generate typed pure policy functions only, and enforce common behavioral fixtures. Do not treat carried JavaScript in Rust comments as a successful generated port.

Suggested implementation: recognize CommonJS require/module.exports as module boundaries, introduce adapter schemas for spawn/exit/status, filesystem reads/atomic writes, environment and timers, and lower only explicitly typed supported signatures to native implementations. Emit an adapter-specific carried reason for unavailable runtime APIs. Keep generation fail closed and add executable fixture tests for argument boundaries and error mapping across Node/Bun and Rust. Existing #220 concerns unbound ESM identifiers; this request covers native runtime semantics and CommonJS specifically.

## Problem

`selfTranslate` (at eb0574d5) roots a module's own helpers at the crate: a loop becomes `crate::ml_sum_loop1`, and the preludes are `crate::ml::Big` and `crate::ml_array::at`. It also translates a relative import as a path to a sibling module, `use crate::math::{double};`, so the module is meant to be one module of a crate.

These two choices conflict. As a crate root, the module's `use crate::math::…` does not resolve. As a module `crate::sum`, its `crate::ml_sum_loop1` does not resolve. No crate holds both.

```js
import { double } from './math.mjs';

/** @param {number} n @returns {number} */
export function sum(n) {
  let total = 0;
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i += 1) total += double(i);
  return total;
}
```

With `{ imports: { './math.mjs': selfTranslationSignatures(math) } }`, this gives `use crate::math::{double};` and `crate::ml_sum_loop1(n, total, i)`.

## Expected

The module's own items are named relative to the module: `self::ml_sum_loop1`, or `crate::<moduleDirectory>::<module>::…` when the module's name is known. Then the translated modules of a directory compile as one crate.

## Workaround in formal-ai

formal-ai's compile check (`scripts/translate-js-rust.mjs --compile`) assembles each root as one crate of modules. It re-roots each module's own `crate::x` paths as `crate::<module>::x`. This is recorded as `crate-assembly` in `data/meta/translation-workarounds.lino`.

### Problem

When a function has no JSDoc `@param` type, the portable core infers the parameter's type from how the item's own body uses it (`js/src/translation/javascript-infer.js`, main at `679a3b3c`). A parameter that nothing in the body constrains becomes a Number. A parameter the body iterates with `for…of` or indexes becomes an array of strings. Self-translation checks each item alone, so the callers elsewhere in the module, which do constrain the type, are never seen. The Rust signature is then a guess, and the item is still reported as `translated`.

From link-assistant/formal-ai's modules, as they were before JSDoc types were added:

```js
function pluralize(count, singular, plural) {
  return count === 1 ? singular : plural;
}
// called as pluralize(n, 'file', 'files')
```

```rust
pub fn pluralize(count: f64, singular: f64, plural: f64) -> f64 { … }
```

```js
function escape(text) {
  let out = '';
  for (const character of text) { … }
  return out;
}
// called with a string
```

```rust
pub fn escape(text: Vec<String>) -> String { … }
```

Both compile. Neither can be called the way the JavaScript is called, and nothing in the item list or the output says the types were guessed.

### Consumer need

link-assistant/formal-ai counts translated items as progress and ratchets the count ([link-assistant/formal-ai#1188](https://github.com/link-assistant/formal-ai/pull/1188), `data/meta/js-rust-translation.lino`). Its first measurement found 17 translated items whose signatures were inferred. 9 of them contradicted their callers: six took string parameters typed `f64` (one compares two kind names with `<`), and three took a string typed `Vec<String>`. With JSDoc types added, 5 of the 9 translate with `String` parameters, and the other 4 are carried (string indexing, iteration and ordering are outside the core). That is the honest result. The consumer now flags translated items that lack `@param` types, but it has to detect them heuristically from the source lines.

### Request

1. Refuse a parameter whose type nothing constrains (`type: declare the type of <name> with JSDoc`), instead of choosing Number. Or emit it generically.
2. When self-translating a module, infer a parameter's type from the module's call sites as well, or refuse when the item's own body is the only evidence.
3. Record in the item list (and in the translated marker) which parameters had inferred types, so a consumer can require declared types without parsing the source.

Acceptance: the two functions above, unannotated, are either carried with a "declare the type" reason or translated with `&str`/`String` parameters, and a corpus case pins it.

