## Construct

Methods of strings beyond `startsWith/endsWith/includes`, the case maps and the trims. They are refused with `method call .<name>(): methods of values are outside the portable core`. Two related gaps: `String.prototype.length` is refused because the portable string keeps no UTF-16 length, and `for (let c of text)`, iteration by code point, fails type inference (`one value is used as a string and as an array`).

## Count in formal-ai

| construct | here | wider measurement |
|---|---:|---:|
| `.split()` | 53 | 169 |
| `.slice()` (strings and arrays) | 40 | 130 |
| `.indexOf()` (strings and arrays) | 26 | 71 |
| `.replace()` (string or regex pattern) | 23 | 80 |
| `.codePointAt()` | 15 | 7 |
| `.replaceAll()` | 9 | 12 |
| `.lastIndexOf()` | 7 | 7 |
| `.repeat()` | 3 | 2 |
| `.padStart()` | 1 | 3 |
| `one value is used as a string and as an array` (iteration, `.length`) | 6 | 12 |

Together that is about **183** carried items here.

## Minimal input

```js
/** @param {string} line @returns {string[]} */
export function fields(line) {
  return line.split(',');
}

/** @param {string} text @returns {string} */
export function afterFirstComma(text) {
  const index = text.indexOf(',');
  return index < 0 ? text : text.slice(index + 1);
}

/** @param {string} text @returns {number} */
export function countA(text) {
  let count = 0;
  for (let character of text) {
    if (character === 'a') count += 1;
  }
  return count;
}
```

## Expected Rust

```rust
pub fn fields(line: String) -> Vec<String> {
    line.split(',').map(String::from).collect()
}

pub fn afterFirstComma(text: String) -> String {
    let index = crate::ml_string::index_of(&text, ",");
    if index < 0f64 { text } else { crate::ml_string::slice(&text, index + 1f64) }
}

pub fn countA(text: String) -> f64 {
    let mut count = 0f64;
    for character in text.chars() {
        if character.to_string() == "a" {
            count += 1f64;
        }
    }
    count
}
```

## Suggested direction

The UTF-16 question that keeps `.length` out also governs `indexOf`/`slice`. Two options:

1. Emit prelude helpers (`ml_string::index_of`, `slice`, `length`) that count UTF-16 code units exactly as JavaScript does, so behavior is faithful on any input.
2. Offer a declared code-point mode (a decorator or option), for projects that never index by UTF-16 offsets.

`split` with a non-empty string separator, `replaceAll` and `replace` with a string pattern, and `repeat` behave the same on any encoding and need no such choice. `padStart` pads to a UTF-16 length, so it belongs with `slice`.

