## Construct

Methods of arrays, refused with `method call .<name>(): methods of values are outside the portable core`. The portable core already reads arrays (`length`, indexing, `for…of`) and builds them with spread. What is missing is the standard library that idiomatic JavaScript uses instead of index loops.

## Count in formal-ai

Each carried item counts once, by its first refused construct:

| construct | here | wider measurement |
|---|---:|---:|
| `.find()` | 59 | 57 |
| `.map()` | 48 | 97 |
| `.filter()` | 44 | 69 |
| `.some()` | 42 | 85 |
| `Array.from` | 40 | 66 |
| `.push()` | 34 | 84 |
| `.every()` | 12 | 12 |
| `.join()` | 11 | 19 |
| `.flatMap()` | 10 | 7 |
| `.reduce()` | 10 | 13 |
| `Array.isArray` | 7 | 68 |
| `.sort()` | 5 | 2 |
| `.forEach()` | 2 | 19 |

Together that is **324** carried items here. `.slice()` and `.indexOf()` (40 and 26) are shared with strings; see the string-method issue.

## Minimal input

```js
/**
 * @param {string[]} names
 * @param {string} prefix
 * @returns {boolean}
 */
export function anyStartsWith(names, prefix) {
  return names.some((name) => name.startsWith(prefix));
}

/**
 * @param {string[]} names
 * @param {string} prefix
 * @returns {string[]}
 */
export function upperWithPrefix(names, prefix) {
  return names.filter((name) => name.startsWith(prefix)).map((name) => name.toUpperCase());
}

/** @param {string[]} names @returns {string} */
export function joined(names) {
  const out = [];
  for (let i = 0; i < names.length; i += 1) out.push(names[i].trim());
  return out.join(',');
}
```

## Expected Rust

```rust
pub fn anyStartsWith(names: Vec<String>, prefix: String) -> bool {
    names.iter().any(|name| name.starts_with(prefix.as_str()))
}

pub fn upperWithPrefix(names: Vec<String>, prefix: String) -> Vec<String> {
    names
        .iter()
        .filter(|name| name.starts_with(prefix.as_str()))
        .map(|name| name.to_uppercase())
        .collect()
}

pub fn joined(names: Vec<String>) -> String {
    let mut out: Vec<String> = Vec::new();
    for name in &names {
        out.push(crate::ml_string::trim(name));
    }
    out.join(",")
}
```

## Suggested direction

This depends on closures (the arrow-function issue). `.push` on a local array that does not escape before it is complete is a local mutation and can lower to a `let mut` vector, the same way loops already lower. `.find` returns `Option<T>` (see the `null` issue).

